nolinemap.nw   Source of two companion tools for languages, like
               Python, whose indentation notangle's -L option cannot
               preserve (see issue #41):

linemark       a noweb filter that inserts source-location comments
               (default `# %L "%F"') at the start of each code chunk
               and after each line that uses a chunk.  Tangle WITHOUT
               -L and indentation survives.

nolinemap      runs a command (or filters stdin) and rewrites
               messages that point into tangled files so that they
               point into the .nw sources, translating columns too.
               Works for compilers, interpreters, linters, editor
               quickfix streams.  Also understands #line directives,
               so it works for notangle -L output as well.  Installed
               also under the alias `noerr', after the old Perl tool
               it supersedes.

example.nw     test fixture; run `make test' (needs python3).

Man pages linemark.1 and nolinemap.1 tangle from nolinemap.nw too;
`make install LIB=... MAN=...' installs them (skipped when MAN is
empty, as when driven by contrib/Makefile), with noerr.1 linked to
nolinemap.1.  Both commands answer -h.

Typical use:
    notangle -filter linemark foo.nw > foo.py
    nolinemap python3 foo.py
